.TH TOYCPU 1
.SH NAME
toycpu \- Little Man Computer-style toy CPU and assembler
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dreammachines/toycpu
.RI [ program.lmc ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Toycpu
is a work-alike of "Bucky's Wristwatch"/"Rock Bottom" from Nelson's
.IR "Computer Lib/Dream Machines" :
how few instructions does something still recognisable as a computer
need? It implements a Little Man Computer-style machine: 100 numbered
mailboxes (000\(en999 each), one accumulator, and ten instructions
(add, subtract, load, store, an unconditional jump, two conditional
jumps, input, output, halt).
.PP
.I Program.lmc
defaults to
.BR /lib/toycpu/countdown.lmc .
Assembly source is one mailbox per line,
.RI '[ label ]
.IR MNEMONIC " [" operand ]',
with forward-referenced labels resolved in a first pass. The front
panel shows a live dump of all 100 mailboxes with the program
counter's own mailbox marked
.RB ` -> '.
.PP
Keys:
.B s
steps one instruction,
.B r
runs to completion or the next
.B INP
that has no input queued,
.B t
resets to the start of the loaded program, and an input box
(only meaningful once the machine is blocked on
.BR INP )
lets you supply the next value.
.SH FILES
.TF /lib/toycpu/countdown.lmc
.TP
.B /lib/toycpu/addtwo.lmc
Minimal INP/STA/INP/ADD/OUT/HLT smoke test.
.TP
.B /lib/toycpu/countdown.lmc
A real loop: reads a number and counts it down to zero.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/dreammachines/toycpu.b
.br
.B /appl/dreammachines/plugin/toycpu.b
.br
.B /module/dreammachines/toycpu.m
